#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078250 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078392 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c44fafc7-d2e5-584e-8812-6b6983b91cdc" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Trigonometric Ratio by Using the Pythagorean Theorem to Solve for the Hypotenuse"}] :question/id #uuid "8ea6aa9d-bdb3-58d5-9182-8f70176f7060" :question/math-academy-id "e-3242" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\cos L.$\n\n![](images/78/78416d3c8ec8a8351ecfe0a4ea81feb05cce29f55dd9ec4bc676e6363dd49ec0.png)" :question/worked-solution "Recall the definition of cosine: \n\n$$\n\\cos L = \\dfrac {\\textrm{adjacent}}{\\textrm{hypotenuse}} = \\dfrac {JL}{KL} = \\dfrac{3\\sqrt{3}}{KL}\n$$\n\nHere, we need to determine the missing side $KL.$ The trick is to use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nKL &= \\sqrt{ JK^2 + JL^2 } \\\n&= \\sqrt{ (3)^2 + (3\\sqrt3)^2 } \\\n&= \\sqrt{  9 + 27 } \\\n&= \\sqrt{36}\\\n&= 6\\\n\t}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\\cos L &= \\dfrac{3\\sqrt{3}}{KL} \\[5pt] \n&= \\dfrac{3\\sqrt 3}{6} \\[5pt] \n&=\\dfrac {\\sqrt 3} 2.\n}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078252 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078393 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a83e174-ab83-5bf6-898e-0fe5cddc8ef4" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Trigonometric Ratio by Using the Pythagorean Theorem to Solve for a Leg"}] :question/id #uuid "ab766585-35c3-5c75-bb18-d7d22b505079" :question/math-academy-id "e-3244" :question/problem "Find the sine of $\\angle Y.$\n\n![](images/e0/e0fb47c85d56c314389a0a334029b3b769f13ddcca6ab15a9021cf6361e4b1ce.png)" :question/worked-solution "Recall the definition of sine: \n\n$$\n\\sin Y = \\dfrac {\\textrm{opposite}}{\\textrm{hypotenuse}} = \\dfrac {XZ}{YZ} = \\dfrac{XZ}{61}\n$$\n\nHere, we need to determine the missing side $XZ.$ The trick is to use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nXZ &= \\sqrt{ YZ^2 - XY^2 } \\\n&= \\sqrt{ 61^2 - 60^2 } \\\n&= \\sqrt{ 3721- 3600 } \\\n&= \\sqrt{121 }\\\n&= 11\n\t}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\sin Y&= \\dfrac{XZ}{61} = \\dfrac{11}{61}.\n\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078254 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078394 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dc4cb42-6295-5484-b37e-0c557c4f6073" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Trigonometric Ratio by Using the Pythagorean Theorem with Decimals"}] :question/id #uuid "cc59ecc0-e886-5e7d-8fbe-c63df5995d3d" :question/math-academy-id "e-3246" :question/problem "Calculate $\\tan K.$\n\n![](images/fb/fbac8700b860cbe8a60f2c24a5ab7ee9e7adfda584b1077edf208b0dcd16744e.png)" :question/worked-solution "Recall the definition of tangent: \n\n$$\n\\tan K = \\dfrac {\\textrm{opposite}}{\\textrm{adjacent}} = \\dfrac {JL}{JK} = \\dfrac{JL}{7.5}\n$$\n\nHere, we need to determine the missing side $JL.$ The trick is to use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nJL &= \\sqrt{ KL^2 - JK^2 } \\\n&= \\sqrt{ 12.5^2 - 7.5^2 } \\\n&= \\sqrt{ 156.25-56.25 } \\\n&= \\sqrt{100 }\\\n&= 10\n\t}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\tan K&= \\dfrac{JL}{7.5}\\[5pt]\n &= \\dfrac{10}{7.5}  \\[5pt]\n &=\\dfrac 4 3.\n\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540063 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078392 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c44fafc7-d2e5-584e-8812-6b6983b91cdc" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Trigonometric Ratio by Using the Pythagorean Theorem to Solve for the Hypotenuse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0add5691-f942-5b35-b192-c1da1630a40e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539501} {:answer/id #uuid "1207c2a1-1cfe-5805-b5da-493d5d4dc80f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539514} {:answer/id #uuid "4badf008-2cee-5e94-824b-287c9932a897" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539616} {:answer/id #uuid "5a9defc5-4f52-5829-b8db-b07e93aef54d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539632} {:answer/id #uuid "6b7942ad-8b0a-561d-a64b-d2217edba6dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1207c2a1-1cfe-5805-b5da-493d5d4dc80f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539514} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c0219dc-9ab7-51bd-a54a-d85c12bf6485" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539924}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "78a31293-8e99-54e0-b99f-691a4b369ca7" :question/math-academy-id "q-121995" :question/problem "![](images/18/182a88f3f8f379dd35228cbb6ebe5c34d9761608a6a222710f50d8f272d3cf1e.png)\n\nCalculate $\\operatorname{cos}⁡R.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the definition of cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡R=\\frac{\\text{adjacent}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}=\\frac{QR}{PR}=\\frac{2}{PR}\n$$\n\nHere, we need to determine the missing length $PR.$ The trick is to use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PR & =\\sqrt{P{Q}^{2}+Q{R}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡R & =\\frac{2}{PR}=\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540105 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078392 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c44fafc7-d2e5-584e-8812-6b6983b91cdc" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Trigonometric Ratio by Using the Pythagorean Theorem to Solve for the Hypotenuse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "047a8d6f-0971-5830-aa95-f722e6cf572a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539486} {:answer/id #uuid "61917919-af64-52dd-97f7-0796668d8d30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539643} {:answer/id #uuid "9f287caf-a6b9-5406-98e8-376345c62c95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539745} {:answer/id #uuid "a1c442f1-f117-5be2-9dca-f353896f1aa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539750} {:answer/id #uuid "eb784457-19d3-514d-981d-cf13feed00a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539876}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "047a8d6f-0971-5830-aa95-f722e6cf572a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539486} :answer-field/id #uuid "afd44c8a-35d4-5999-8bc8-aec4ecd757f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539983}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e6b2c405-4949-514d-b4c7-24a8d0d745b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-122005" :question/problem "In a right triangle $△LMN,$ $∠M$ is a right angle, $MN=\\sqrt{5}$ and $LM=\\sqrt{11}.$ Determine the cosine of $∠N.$" :question/worked-solution "The following triangle illustrates the information given.\n\n![](images/df/df7dcd343dd2676cb38b7df3c2d8cb599fed45b9c0b25194609effe2bf27a6f7.png)\n\nRecall the definition of cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡N=\\frac{\\text{adjacent}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}=\\frac{MN}{LN}=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{LN}\n$$\n\nHere, we need to determine the missing length $LN.$ The trick is to use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}LN & =\\sqrt{M{N}^{2}+L{M}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}+(\\sqrt{11})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5+11} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡N & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{LN}=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540081 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078393 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a83e174-ab83-5bf6-898e-0fe5cddc8ef4" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Trigonometric Ratio by Using the Pythagorean Theorem to Solve for a Leg"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7559fa9a-aeb1-53b4-a743-b8b0a1e0597c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539675} {:answer/id #uuid "8b5c1919-5049-5ae5-89d4-605cdbb464f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539714} {:answer/id #uuid "df818243-287d-525c-baf3-bdf1e0c459d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539863} {:answer/id #uuid "e2cc5e62-162d-5e1f-9f7d-d682ac2eccc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539866} {:answer/id #uuid "f8a1f3ca-7358-58e7-9427-e7284d90f8c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539900}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8a1f3ca-7358-58e7-9427-e7284d90f8c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539900} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e6afa06-33da-5d44-9148-766078857a10" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539970}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aec65431-c1ff-5f84-a471-b95024c34089" :question/math-academy-id "q-122045" :question/problem "In a right triangle $△FGH,$ $∠H$ is a right angle, $FH=2\\sqrt{2}$ and $FG=6.$ Find the cosine of $∠G.$" :question/worked-solution "The following triangle illustrates the information given.\n\n![](images/e5/e585e89d11dea316c5483eeb2b2fc4750084409651ecf838e65a0d09e7bcc97f.png)\n\nRecall the definition of cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡G=\\frac{\\text{adjacent}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}=\\frac{GH}{FG}=\\frac{GH}{6}\n$$\n\nHere, we need to determine the missing length $GH.$ The trick is to use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}GH & =\\sqrt{F{G}^{2}-F{H}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}-(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36-8} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{28} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡G & =\\frac{GH}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{7}}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540033 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078394 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dc4cb42-6295-5484-b37e-0c557c4f6073" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Trigonometric Ratio by Using the Pythagorean Theorem with Decimals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30f116f5-65c3-5da0-b526-fc463090f7cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539573} {:answer/id #uuid "4ba7de20-c492-55d4-8758-69e5179b70b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539615} {:answer/id #uuid "84697758-83bc-5e35-9734-bb613b80e494" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539697} {:answer/id #uuid "caf2e0b5-8b96-5e09-aeca-821cf0b1312d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539826} {:answer/id #uuid "d798b0c6-c031-55f2-9b2a-7bb43c0f75c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539850}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d798b0c6-c031-55f2-9b2a-7bb43c0f75c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539850} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a366115-f348-5b02-b72c-bbac02b13817" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539943}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1fdf769a-b9f2-5675-a0ff-59f0108ca843" :question/math-academy-id "q-122173" :question/problem "![](images/bd/bdc0ea077dce75d6de1250ee6cf2e6dbd119b5124330c9a92edc071e475d0755.png)\n\nFind the sine of $∠U.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the definition of sine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡U=\\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}=\\frac{ST}{SU}=\\frac{ST}{12.5}\n$$\n\nHere, we need to determine the missing length $ST.$ The trick is to use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ST & =\\sqrt{S{U}^{2}-T{U}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(12.5)^{2}-(7.5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{156.25-56.25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{100} \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡U & =\\frac{ST}{12.5} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{12.5} \\\\  & =\\frac{100}{125} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078740 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078394 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dc4cb42-6295-5484-b37e-0c557c4f6073" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Trigonometric Ratio by Using the Pythagorean Theorem with Decimals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9aa19083-4794-58ee-8fad-4ca0e69496d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075317} {:answer/id #uuid "dc53aa94-495a-5de2-ac52-713c00c8250d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075318} {:answer/id #uuid "dfbd7e1a-d670-5049-be73-b31bceca763c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075319} {:answer/id #uuid "e7eb01b0-bbd4-5b07-a9a3-831c67740f92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075320} {:answer/id #uuid "56ab04b7-2119-5221-9469-344254399a8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9aa19083-4794-58ee-8fad-4ca0e69496d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075317} :answer-field/id #uuid "26d5018e-19d4-5b5f-aa3f-04252b407275" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075316}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5ae01ab6-b771-5f83-a86c-608699d71684" :question/math-academy-id "q-14644" :question/problem "![](images/f9/f9f59d4328ad43e6e342da3cf9e87ccd5dfe33b97c46424e7fcc2b9f562fc452.png)\n\nFind the tangent of $∠N.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Recall the definition of tangent: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡N=\\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{adjacent}}=\\frac{OM}{MN}=\\frac{OM}{2.5}\n$$\n\nHere, we need to determine the missing side $OM.$ The trick is to use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}OM & =\\sqrt{O{N}^{2}-M{N}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{6.5}^{2}-{2.5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{42.25-6.25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡N & =\\frac{OM}{2.5} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{2.5} \\\\  & =\\frac{60}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540061 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078394 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dc4cb42-6295-5484-b37e-0c557c4f6073" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Trigonometric Ratio by Using the Pythagorean Theorem with Decimals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0de6fd12-2f07-5755-8d3a-6f1d8b0319e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539508} {:answer/id #uuid "18228661-d120-5681-bd34-2bcd4f93d785" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539527} {:answer/id #uuid "34c6f76d-84a5-5884-8ea5-5a0513000b47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539577} {:answer/id #uuid "7e77ab69-4e00-57d5-a0d0-3b4f04f4d6a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539688} {:answer/id #uuid "fc7db012-77bb-5d89-bd59-a55587f61d54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539906}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0de6fd12-2f07-5755-8d3a-6f1d8b0319e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539508} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d249db5-57d3-569f-92f5-17914774a070" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539969}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7705945a-9fcd-5876-a71d-6b7d8aba9b73" :question/math-academy-id "q-67554" :question/problem "![](images/de/de81dfe9ea7efd41a7ca5b576452c62491ab438dbb0b75eb7c7a6c13a903fe6f.png)\n\nDetermine the cosine of $∠Q.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the definition of cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡Q=\\frac{\\text{adjacent}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}=\\frac{QR}{PQ}=\\frac{QR}{8.5}\n$$\n\nHere, we need to determine the missing side $QR.$ The trick is to use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}QR & =\\sqrt{P{Q}^{2}-P{R}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(8.5)^{2}-(7.5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{72.25-56.25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡Q & =\\frac{QR}{8.5} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{8.5} \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{85} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234385 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078393 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a83e174-ab83-5bf6-898e-0fe5cddc8ef4" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Trigonometric Ratio by Using the Pythagorean Theorem to Solve for a Leg"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "248f2cde-b408-5ab0-97e4-f1139e42032a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539549} {:answer/id #uuid "2786aa44-fd8d-51d2-a04a-19154dd9d613" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{11}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539555} {:answer/id #uuid "42add563-41cd-5c7b-8e51-c9a40fe536c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{22}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539601} {:answer/id #uuid "b9564023-fd38-51dd-acff-be1c03307b8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539802} {:answer/id #uuid "d919f955-2be1-55b1-959e-4365f6131cc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{11}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539852}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d919f955-2be1-55b1-959e-4365f6131cc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{11}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539852} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ccf3b06-8e27-5175-a63c-45b18215cccf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539946}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "021c99cd-4ec5-510e-b0c7-e32ccd554ad9" :question/math-academy-id "q-76560" :question/problem "In a right triangle $△XYZ,$ $∠X$ is a right angle, $XZ=\\sqrt{5}$ and $YZ=7.$ Find the cosine of $∠Y.$" :question/worked-solution "The following triangle illustrates the information given.\n\n![](images/ea/ea0502e8b261358c7b55c60df02474c5b39a75cbb104d6ba3efb40300294ef56.png)\n\nRecall the definition of cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡Y=\\frac{\\text{adjacent}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}=\\frac{XY}{YZ}=\\frac{XY}{7}\n$$\n\nHere, we need to determine the missing side $XY.$ The trick is to use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{Y{Z}^{2}-X{Z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{7}^{2}-(\\sqrt{5})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{49-5} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{44} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{11}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡Y & =\\frac{XY}{7}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{11}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540114 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078394 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dc4cb42-6295-5484-b37e-0c557c4f6073" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Trigonometric Ratio by Using the Pythagorean Theorem with Decimals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41461aa7-43de-55b4-80f5-ac43667106ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539596} {:answer/id #uuid "76bffbe9-0710-5f13-8f49-ff8692ccf9d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539677} {:answer/id #uuid "ad6f986b-c2cd-55e4-a5d3-6fbb550bf5f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539774} {:answer/id #uuid "d13eb98c-1c94-5c76-9b4d-87ec446e756d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539838} {:answer/id #uuid "fb73bb44-7d7d-557b-b4d7-55b44301f620" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539903}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d13eb98c-1c94-5c76-9b4d-87ec446e756d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539838} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e20ba3b-2ce4-5c79-9503-04b8fbe7a3c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539975}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fc60b6be-fd37-55aa-981e-4006b67e2bf9" :question/math-academy-id "q-76563" :question/problem "In a right triangle $△OMN,$ $∠M$ is a right angle, $MN=1.8$ and $ON=3.$ Determine $\\operatorname{cos}⁡O.$" :question/worked-solution "The following triangle illustrates the information given.\n\n![](images/5c/5c980bff90a516627618909a863b4919dbb10be1b674911cae663f3f7c54f3fb.png)\n\nRecall the definition of cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡O=\\frac{\\text{adjacent}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}=\\frac{OM}{ON}=\\frac{OM}{3}\n$$\n\nHere, we need to determine the missing side $OM.$ The trick is to use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}OM & =\\sqrt{O{N}^{2}-M{N}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}-{1.8}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9-3.24} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5.76} \\\\  & =2.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡O & =\\frac{OM}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2.4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{30} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
